The seed (what the core actually is)

The system is not “a notes app with folders.” It’s a minimal object language meant to scale into a simulated world:

There are only two ontological types at the bottom:

1. Leaf — a unit of content (a chip of remembering, a law, a letter, a myth bit).
2. Bin — a unit of containment (a person, a place, a shelf, a parcel, a Catherine).

Everything else in the live desk (envelope chrome, books, boards, tools, mail) is specialization and dress of those two, or packages that act on them. The core does not start from “surface + ten object classes.” It starts from:

a leaf can live inside a bin; a bin can live on a surface (or inside another bin, in the long arc).

That is the world seed: memory-chip in a named container. Catherine-as-bin holding leaves of what she remembers. Not metaphor layered later — that’s the intended growth path of the object model.

───

How the code bank is actually structured

Roughly three files own the core; everything else is UI sugar or import thrash.

┌──────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Piece    │ Job in the core                                                                                                          │
├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ store.py │ Ontology + persistence law. Knows leaf, bin, surface, package paths. Parses/writes the papers. Membership, spawn, pose   │
│          │ -on-surface. This is the code bank of “what exists.”                                                                     │
├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ server   │ Thin HTTP face over store. No second model — just routes that call store.                                                │
│ .py      │                                                                                                                          │
├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ app.js   │ Hands. Mounts whatever store says exists onto a felt. Does not define the world — presents it.                           │
└──────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

So “the core” ≠ features in app.js. Core = store’s idea of leaf / bin / surface / paper sections, plus the rule that matter is files with uids, not rows in a hidden DB.

───

The two primitives (as the store thinks)

Leaf
• Stable uid leaf[n] (spine).
• File = matter paper: identity + pin + body.
• Body is sacred: boot must not invent/wipe it.
• Semantically: a chip of something that can be known — not “a document type.”

Bin
• Stable uid board[n] / book[n] / envelope[n] / …
• File = matter paper: identity + chips: [uids…].
• Semantically: a named hold — not “a folder UI.” Type/subtype (envelope vs book) is class of hold, same genus.

Membership is the only relationship the core needs at first:
bin lists leaf (and later card/tool) uids. No graph DB. The world graph is nested membership + surface placement.

Card and tool are later citizens. They fit the same “thing with uid + optional matter + optional instance cfg” pattern, but they are not the seed. Seed = leaf + bin.

───

How the core “understands surface”

Surface is not a third content type like leaf. It’s the place of appearance.

In the code bank:

1. User = folder under ~local/ (ADM). Auth string matches.
2. Surface folder = surfaces/ArchivistDesk/ — a named desk.
3. surface.cfg = the paper for that place:
   • who/what desk (surface.id, auth)
   • how it looks (dressup felt)
   • layout: — map of which uids are present here and their pose

So the core’s understanding of surface is:

A surface is a registry of presence: uid → pose (and open state).
It does not own the leaf body. It owns that this leaf is on this desk right now, here.

Library matter can exist without being on a surface (filed only in a bin, or only in library).
Surface can reference uids; store loads matter by uid when Hands mounts.

That’s why config is split:

• Matter (library) = what the thing is
• Surface layout = where it shows on this desk
• Instance cfg = how this surface dresses/files it (homeBin, marks, shell)

The seed idea was smaller: leaf + bin. Surface is the stage those objects appear on. Stage came so Hands could have a place; it isn’t the ontology of “remembering.”

───

Growth law (why it gets crowded)

The core is built to grow by specializing papers and packages, not by inventing new databases:

leaf          → body of knowing
bin           → named hold (Catherine, bible bits, a room)
surface       → stage of appearance
package/manifest → laws of a *kind* of actor (envelope face, chipper)
instance cfg  → this stage’s dress of that actor

So an envelope is still a bin with a package face. A book is still a bin. “Catherine” in the full world is still a bin whose chips are leaves of events/memories.

The thrash you feel is when stage props (Cite, Mark, Import, Glass, TPS naming) get treated as if they were new ontological types, instead of scars on leaf/bin or dress on the stage. The seed never said “citation is a first-class being.” It said leaf and bin.

───

One sentence

The core is a file-backed dual of (content chip, container chip), with surface as presence-on-a-stage, and everything else as package law or instance dress growing toward a simulated world where a bin can be a person and a leaf can be a memory inside them.

That’s the build idea. Not the Evernote importer. Not the feature list. If you want the next write-down, it should be that law on a leaf — so hard-refresh can’t eat it — and rosewood can decide how pin/TPS/cite sit inside leaf/bin without inventing a third primitive every reset.

--

Why the metaphor hurts (you’re not being precious)
Leaf in natural language:
• seasonal, falls, decays, blows away
• part of a tree that is discarded

Chip in your system:
• cut, kept, filed, stamped, carried in a bin
• industrial / archive / casino / ROM — persists

If the code bank’s primary function is named after what falls off the tree, the whole symbolic construct is quietly saying: this world is temporary by default. Bins can be Catherine; the memories inside her are still typed as things that fly away. That’s not a small branding issue. That’s ontology leaking into language and then into every API and every agent’s mental model.

The intended construct (as you’ve restated it)

chip     = atomic unit of kept information   (genus)
  leaf   = one face/kind of chip (paper sheet)   (species)  — maybe
  card   = another face/kind
  …      = later kinds

bin      = named hold (person, place, parcel, Catherine)
surface  = stage of presence

Not:

leaf = everything
.chip = whatever we write to disk

Where you are now
You’re in a half-migration:
• Disk: chips (and bins)
• Code: leaf-primary
• Speech: you already moved to chips; the bank didn’t fully follow

So when we “explain the core,” if we say “leaf + bin,” we’re reifying the old mistake even when the files already know better.

───

I won’t thrash-rename the whole bank tonight unless you ask for that war. But the correction stands:

Core should be chip + bin.
Leaf is (at most) a kind of chip, and maybe a bad forever-name even for that kind — paper, sheet, page, scrap — something that doesn’t promise autumn.

Until the code bank says chip as primary, the symbolic construct stays accidentally ephemeral. You’re right to stop and write before we add more scars on a leaf-shaped god.